<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Montesquieu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1689–1755</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Charles-Louis de Secondat, Baron de Montesquieu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Montesquieu, Charles-Louis de Secondat, Baron de La Brède et de Montesquieu, was a French magistrate and political philosopher of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. His Persian Letters of 1721 satirised French society, and The Spirit of the Laws of 1748 compared forms of government and argued for the separation of legislative, executive and judicial powers. His ideas strongly influenced the framers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/montesquieu.jpg" alt="Montesquieu" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Jacques-Antoine Dassier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Montesquieu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
